# Shopify Liquid — Senior Developer Rules (5+ Years)

---

## Directory Structure

- Only these directories are supported: `assets/`, `blocks/`, `config/`, `layout/`, `locales/`, `sections/`, `snippets/`, `templates/`, `templates/customers/`, `templates/metaobject/`. Files outside these are not served or compiled.
- The only file required for a theme to upload is `layout/theme.liquid`. No templates are required, but each page type you want to render needs a matching template.
- `config/` contains exactly two files: `settings_schema.json` (structure) and `settings_data.json` (current values). Global theme settings are accessed in Liquid via `settings.*`, never `section.settings.*`.
- `assets/` supports `.js.liquid` and `.css.liquid` files with access to the `settings` object and Liquid filters. Use for injecting global theme tokens (fonts, colors) into CSS/JS without DOM reads. Use sparingly — these bypass normal asset bundling.

---

## Core Liquid Syntax

- Use `{% liquid %}` tags for multi-line logic to reduce tag clutter.
- Inside `{% liquid %}` blocks, use `echo var` to output values — `{{ var }}` syntax is not valid inside `{% liquid %}`.
- Use whitespace trimming `{{- -}}` / `{%- -%}` intentionally, not by default. Apply only when output whitespace causes layout or JSON issues. Overuse hurts readability without meaningful gain.
- Prefer `{% render %}` over `{% include %}` — `include` is deprecated in OS 2.0.
- `{% render 'snippet', variable: value %}` passes isolated scope. Snippets rendered with `{% render %}` do NOT inherit parent scope — pass all needed vars explicitly.
- Use `{% render 'tag' for product.tags as tag %}` to render a snippet per array item.
- `{{ content_for_header }}` is **required** in `layout/theme.liquid` inside `<head>`. It injects Shopify scripts (apps, hCaptcha, analytics). Do not attempt to parse or modify its output.
- `{{ content_for_layout }}` is **required** in `layout/theme.liquid` between `<body>` and `</body>`. It outputs template content. Shopify CLI will reject saves to `theme.liquid` if either object is missing.
- Use `{% content_for_index %}` in `index.liquid` only if opting out of JSON template for the index page. Standard OS 2.0 themes use `index.json`.

---

## Layouts

- Multiple layout files are supported beyond `theme.liquid`. Assign layouts at the template level: `"layout"` key in JSON templates, `{% layout 'custom' %}` tag in Liquid templates, or `{% layout none %}` to suppress layout entirely.
- Use `{{ template.name }}` in the `<body>` class (`class="template-{{ template.name }}"`) to enable template-scoped CSS rules. This is a documented Shopify pattern — avoids wrapper divs.
- **`checkout.liquid` is deprecated.** It is unsupported for Information, Shipping, and Payment steps. It will be sunset for Thank You and Order Status pages on **August 28, 2025**. Checkout customization now belongs to Checkout UI Extensions. Do not create or extend `checkout.liquid`.

---

## Templates (OS 2.0)

- Use JSON templates (`templates/*.json`) for all standard page types. Liquid templates are appropriate only where dynamic rendering without section infrastructure is required.
- Templates that **must** be Liquid (cannot be JSON): `gift_card.liquid`, `robots.txt.liquid`, `password.liquid`.
- `metaobject/{type}.json` is a valid, first-class template type (e.g., `templates/metaobject/book.json`). When a metaobject definition has the web page capability enabled, each entry renders via this template.
- A theme can have a maximum of **1000 JSON templates** total across all types.
- A JSON template is a data manifest. It contains only section references and a layout declaration. All logic belongs in sections — there is no place for Liquid in a JSON template.
- Use alternate templates (`product.[name].json`, `page.[name].json`) for page-specific layouts.
- **`customers/` Liquid templates are deprecated.** Do not include `customers/login`, `customers/account`, `customers/register`, etc. Publishing a theme without them automatically upgrades merchants to the new customer accounts experience. Add the `<shopify-account>` web component to the theme header for sign-in flows instead.
- Never put business logic directly in JSON templates — delegate to sections.

---

## Sections & Blocks (OS 2.0)

- Every section MUST have a `{% schema %}` block at the bottom.
- A JSON template or section group can render up to **25 sections**. Each section can have up to **50 blocks**. Exceeding these limits silently drops content — design with these hard limits in mind.
- Sections have isolated variable scope. Variables created outside a section are not accessible within it. `section` and `block` objects are not accessible outside their section — the only exception is snippets rendered inside the section, which can access the parent `section` and `block` objects.
- Use `section.settings.*` — never hardcode copy or colors that editors should control.
- Use `enabled_on` or `disabled_on` in section schema to restrict where a section can be added. A section without these keys can be added to any template, including cart or checkout where it may break layout.
- Schema `"class"` property appends a CSS class to the wrapping `.shopify-section` div. Use it for section-level layout targeting instead of adding inner wrapper elements — keeps DOM shallower.
- Use `"limit": 1` in schema for singleton sections. This is a safeguard, not hard enforcement for all cases — global layout sections (header, footer) are enforced at the platform level.
- Add `{{ block.shopify_attributes }}` on the **outermost wrapper** of a block element — not a nested child. Placing it on an inner element breaks drag-to-reorder in the theme editor.
- Sections without `"presets"` can still be added to a JSON template manually (via file edit or CLI) but cannot be added or removed through the theme editor UI. Both are valid — document the distinction for your team.
- Statically rendered sections (`{% section 'name' %}`) share a single settings instance across all locations. Changing settings in one location changes it everywhere. Prefer JSON template references for anything merchant-configurable.
- Use `"max_blocks"` to prevent editor abuse. Use `"type": "header"` dividers to group settings. Use `"info"` keys to explain complex settings. Always provide `"default"` values.
- Keep schema `"name"` under 25 characters for clean editor display.
- For a single CSS property from a setting, use CSS custom properties on the element. For multiple properties, use CSS class names derived from setting values.

---

## Section Groups

- Section groups are **JSON files in the `sections/` directory** (e.g., `sections/header.json`). They enable merchants to add, remove, and reorder sections in layout areas like header and footer.
- Render a section group in `layout/theme.liquid` using `{% sections 'header-group' %}` (the `sections` tag — plural). Using `{% section %}` instead is a silent failure; the group will not render.
- Section groups and JSON templates each support up to 25 sections.

---

## Theme Blocks (Modern OS 2.0)

- Reusable, nestable blocks live in `/blocks/` as `.liquid` files with their own `{% schema %}`. They are reusable across multiple sections; do not declare `"presets"` in their schema (presets are section-only).
- Render all dynamic child blocks with `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`.
- Render a static block with `{% content_for 'block', type: 'block_name', id: 'unique-id' %}`.
- Blocks rendered statically via `content_for 'block'` must include a `{% doc %}` header.
- `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` renders only dynamic (merchant-configurable) blocks. Static blocks use `{% content_for 'block', ... %}` separately. Both can coexist in the same section.

---

## Snippets & LiquidDoc

- Snippets are stateless — receive all data via render parameters. They do not have access to `section.settings` or `block.settings` unless those values are passed explicitly. This is a frequent bug source when refactoring inline section markup into extracted snippets.
- New custom snippets use the `custom-[name].liquid` prefix.
- Document snippet parameters with `{% doc %}` / `{% enddoc %}`. These blocks are stripped at render time and do not affect output, but they power Shopify CLI type-checking and VS Code IntelliSense. Treat `@param` annotations as mandatory in team environments.

```liquid
{% doc %}
  @param {image} image - The image to render
  @param {string} [url] - Optional destination URL
  @example
  {% render 'custom-image', image: product.featured_image %}
{% enddoc %}
```

---

## Component CSS & JavaScript

- Use `{% stylesheet %}` and `{% javascript %}` tags in sections, blocks, and snippets for colocated assets. These tags are **deduplicated** — if the same snippet is rendered multiple times, styles/scripts are output only once. This deduplication is a key architectural benefit.
- These tags are only supported in `snippets/`, `blocks/`, and `sections/`.
- Liquid is NOT rendered inside `{% stylesheet %}` or `{% javascript %}`. Pass dynamic values via CSS custom properties on HTML elements or `data-*` attributes, then read them in JS.
- Use `{% style %}` only for per-instance CSS custom properties (e.g., a section-specific color from settings). `{% style %}` is output inline per section instance and is **not** deduplicated — do not put component-level styles here.
- To pass Liquid setting values into JavaScript, use a `<script type="application/json">` island or a `data-*` attribute on a DOM element.
- For Dawn custom work, load global overrides via `assets/custom-base.css` and `assets/custom-main.js` in `layout/theme.liquid`.
- Use `| stylesheet_tag` and `| script_tag` filters for external asset files.

---

## Theme Editor JavaScript Integration

- When the theme editor adds, removes, or reorders sections/blocks, HTML is mutated directly on the DOM without a full page reload. JavaScript that depends on `DOMContentLoaded` or page-load events will **not** re-run.
- Listen to Shopify theme editor JavaScript events to reinitialise components correctly:
  - `shopify:section:load` — section added or reloaded
  - `shopify:section:unload` — section removed
  - `shopify:section:select` — section selected in editor
  - `shopify:section:deselect` — section deselected
  - `shopify:block:select` — block selected
  - `shopify:block:deselect` — block deselected
- Use `{% unless request.design_mode %}` to skip heavy JS init in the theme editor when not needed.
- Ensure selected sections/blocks are always visible while selected (e.g., pause a slideshow on block select, scroll to the section).

---

## Section Rendering API

- Sections can be fetched individually via AJAX using `?sections=section-id` without a full page reload. This is the correct pattern for cart drawer updates, dynamic variant swapping, and inline content refreshes.
- Use it instead of re-rendering entire page regions. Multiple sections can be fetched in one request: `?sections=cart-drawer,cart-count`.

---

## Liquid Language Rules

- No parentheses in conditions — use nested `{% if %}` for complex logic.
- No ternary operator — always use `{% if %}`.
- `contains` only works with strings, not objects in arrays.
- By default, Liquid iterates up to 50 items from a collection object. Use `{% paginate %}` for product/collection arrays regardless; use `forloop.limit` for custom arrays.
- Use `{% capture %}` for multi-line string assembly; `{% assign %}` for simple values.
- Prefer `!= blank` over `!= nil` and `!= ""` — `blank` checks nil, empty string, and empty array simultaneously.
- Inside `for` loops, use `forloop.index`, `forloop.first`, `forloop.last`, `forloop.length`.
- Avoid `{% assign %}` inside `{% for %}` loops that accumulate with `| concat` — each concat allocates a new array. Pre-filter outside the loop using `| where`.

---

## Variables & Filters

- Always `| escape` or `| escape_once` for user-generated or dynamic string output in HTML attributes.
- Use `| default: 'fallback'` to guard against nil — never assume a setting exists.
- Use `| strip_html` before character counting or truncating text.
- Use `| handleize` when creating HTML IDs or CSS class slugs from strings.
- Use `| image_url: width: 800 | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', alt: image.alt | escape` — always pass `alt` explicitly through `image_tag`; never rely on the filter's default empty alt.
- Never use deprecated `img_url` or `img_tag` — use `image_url` and `image_tag`.
- `| asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'style'` generates a `<link rel="preload">` only. You still need a separate `| stylesheet_tag` call to actually load the stylesheet. Preload alone does not load the asset.
- Use `| money`, `| money_with_currency`, or `| money_without_trailing_zeros` for prices — never format manually.
- Use `| metafield_tag` only when automatic rendering (formatted date, link for URL type, etc.) is acceptable. For custom markup, access `.value` directly.
- `| sort` is case-sensitive; `| sort_natural` is case-insensitive. Prefer `sort_natural` for merchant-entered or user-generated strings.
- `| where` does strict equality only — no partial matches or ranges. For complex filtering, loop manually with `{% for %}` + `{% if %}` + `| concat` or defer to JavaScript.
- Avoid storing large objects in Liquid `assign` inside loops — prefer direct access.

---

## Metafields & Metaobjects

- Metafield access in Liquid: `product.metafields.namespace.key` returns the **metafield object**. Use `.value` to get the actual value: `product.metafields.namespace.key.value`.
- Always check `!= blank` before rendering: `{% if product.metafields.namespace.key != blank %}`.
- For metaobject references on products/variants, the metafield `type` is `metaobject_reference`. Access the metaobject with `.value`, then fields with `.field('key').value` — not `.fields.key.value` (that is REST Admin syntax, not Liquid).
- Define metafield types in Shopify Admin — never cast manually.

---

## Forms

- Always use `{% form 'type' %}` — never hand-roll form actions.
- `{% form 'product' %}` requires the product object: `{% form 'product', product %}`. Omitting it defaults to the first product in context — a subtle bug in multi-product section layouts.
- Common types: `product`, `contact`, `cart`, `customer_login`, `create_customer`, `localization`, `new_comment`.
- Display validation errors with `{{ form.errors | default_errors }}`.
- For custom error UI, iterate: `{% for field in form.errors %}` and access `form.errors.messages[field]`.
- Pair every input with a `<label>` using `for`/`id` or `aria-label`.

---

## Performance

- LCP/hero images: `loading: 'eager'` and `fetchpriority: 'high'` — never lazy-load above-the-fold heroes.
- Below-the-fold images: `loading: 'lazy'`.
- Avoid rendering large arrays server-side in Liquid. For >50 items, paginate or defer to client-side filtering via JSON data islands.
- Minimize section count per page. Each section adds a Liquid render pass. Pages with 20+ sections with complex schemas impact TTFB. Use nested blocks over separate sections for content-heavy layouts.
- Minimize Liquid logic in `layout/theme.liquid` — keep it lean.

---

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA)

- All `<img>` must have `alt="{{ image.alt | escape }}"`. Decorative images: `alt=""` and `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Icon-only buttons MUST have `aria-label="..."` or `<span class="visually-hidden">...</span>`.
- Use `role="status"` and `aria-live="polite"` for cart count and dynamic updates.
- Focus management: `tabindex="-1"` on modal containers, `.focus()` via JS after open.
- Never remove `outline` on focus without a visible replacement.
- Use semantic HTML: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`.

---

## Internationalisation (i18n)

- Always use `{{ 'key' | t }}` for user-facing strings — never hardcode English text.
- Add storefront strings to `locales/en.default.json`. Add schema label strings to `locales/en.default.schema.json` using `t:namespace.key` in schema JSON. These are **separate files** — do not mix them.
- Use hierarchical snake_case keys (max 3 levels), sentence case for values.
- Use `{{ 'key' | t: variable: value }}` for interpolated translations.
- Use `{{ 'key' | t: count: number }}` for pluralization. Always define all required plural subkeys (`one`, `other`, `zero` where applicable) — missing subkeys silently fall back to the raw key string.

---

## Custom File Naming Convention

- Custom sections: `sections/custom-[name].liquid`
- Custom snippets: `snippets/custom-[name].liquid`
- Custom CSS: `assets/custom-base.css`, `assets/custom-[feature].css`
- Custom JS: `assets/custom-main.js`, `assets/custom-[feature].js`
- NEVER modify Dawn's original files directly — always extend or override.